{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 44, :comments ["<b>And you shall sanctify yourselves and you shall be holy, for I am holy.</b> This holiness, for Israel more than for the other nations, is nothing but through the practice of Torah and the commandments, for with Torah and commandments the power of the intellect is strengthened and the power of desire is weakened. And know that desire is strong by its nature, and behold, in man it precedes his intellect, for desire is implanted in him from the beginning of his youth, when he is drawn after physical pleasures, and with them he goes and grows until it overcomes the intellect for whose sake man was created. Moreover, desire is joined to the powers of the natures and to the elements, and it has many supporters and helpers — namely, foods and the rest of the physical desires. But the intellect, which is a purely spiritual essence and is derived from the supernal world, is a stranger in the world of thick and coarse bodies; and because it is a stranger, it has no supporter or helper — rather, everything is against it. And it is fitting that the intellect should weaken and desire should overcome it, because of two factors: the first, because desire in man precedes the intellect; and the second, because desire has supporters while the intellect does not. Therefore man needed Torah and commandments to strengthen the power of the intellect and to weaken the power of desire. And because of this we were commanded regarding forbidden foods, the commandments of forbidden relations, and the commandments of prayer, fasting, charity, and deeds of lovingkindness — all of which serve to weaken the power of desire. And this is what our Rabbis of blessed memory said in the Sifrei: 'And you shall sanctify yourselves' — this is the holiness of a commandment; 'and you shall be holy' — afterwards. And the intention in this statement is that through the commandments the power of desire is weakened and the power of the intellect, for whose sake man was created, is strengthened." "<b>Furthermore,</b> one may interpret והתקדשתם in the sense of separation (perishut), for one who separates and withdraws from the desires of the world is called holy. And so our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted in Torat Kohanim: 'And you shall sanctify yourselves and you shall be holy — just as I am holy, so shall you be holy; just as I am separated (perush), so shall you be separated (perushim).' And the meaning is that a person should separate himself from desires and not partake of them except as necessary, for if a person is drawn after food and drink and intercourse excessively, he destroys his soul and his body, and he is called in the language of the Torah a fool (כסיל) and a simpleton (אויל). And whoever diminishes even what is necessary and separates himself from what is entirely permitted — he is the one called holy. For if he fills his soul with permitted desires, he will be among the drunkards of wine and the gluttons of flesh, and he will be a degraded person (נבל) and a transgressor of the Torah. Therefore this verse was needed to say והתקדשתם, to warn concerning separation — that a person should fence himself in and separate from an abundance of food, drink, and what is permitted, and he should also separate from improper speech, in the manner of what is written (Proverbs 21:23): ״שומר פיו ולשונו״ — 'who guards his mouth and his tongue,' and so forth. This is what the Ramban of blessed memory wrote. And so we find Solomon, peace be upon him, who warned concerning forbidden speech, for he said (Proverbs 15:2): ״לשון חכמים תיטיב דעת ופי כסילים יביע אולת״, and immediately after it: ״בכל מקום עיני ה' צופות״ and so forth — explaining that the providence of HaShem is in every place, and He repays recompense for folly and for abhorrent speech. And this is what the Prophet said (Isaiah 9:16): ״על כן על בחוריו לא ישמח ה' וכל פה דובר נבלה״ — he mentioned the young men because that is their way. And our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: 'whoever degrades his mouth — they deepen Gehenna for him, as it is said (Proverbs 22:14): ״שוחה עמוקה פי זרה זעום ה' יפל שם״.' And so we find Ezekiel the Prophet, who prophesied punishment and retribution concerning improper speech, and he informed us that the Holy One, blessed be He, exercises providence over this — for he said concerning the punishment of the wicked (Ezekiel 35:10): ״יען אמרך את שני הגוים ואת שתי הארצות לי תהיינה וירשנוה וה' שם היה״ — the verse declares that He, may He be exalted, was there, exercising providence over speech to repay recompense. And in this He further used the expression וה', whose meaning in every place is 'He and His court of justice.'" "<b>And know</b> the opinion of our Rabbis of blessed memory in their Midrash on this verse, that it is a warning to a person to be clean in his body and his hands, for all this falls under the category of separation (perishut). This is what they said: 'והתקדשתם — these are the first waters [ritual handwashing before the meal]; והייתם קדושים — these are the last waters [ritual handwashing after the meal]; כי קדוש אני — this is the oil of anointing [perfume oils at the banquet]; אני ה' אלהיכם — this is the blessing [grace after the meal].' And the intention in this is that a person, at the time of eating and drinking — which is a cause for forgetting the Principal [the awareness of HaShem] — should sanctify himself with three sanctifications: two for the purification of the body, and the third to arouse the power of the soul with a good fragrance. Then one will be worthy to bless the Holy One, blessed be He. And so it says (Psalms 134:2): ״שאו ידיכם קדש וברכו את ה'״ — 'Lift up your hands in holiness and bless HaShem.'"], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:25:14.457514258Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}